---
/**
 * Your network.
 *
 * The objection route. Every buyer's first real doubt is not "does it work" but "our model is
 * not good enough for this", and they are right. The page answers by agreeing, and then
 * showing what the product does about it — which is refuse, and say what it does not know.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
import { SITE, PLANT } from '../data/network';
import { initialState, solve } from '../lib/energise';

const normal = solve(initialState());
const counts = {
  breakers: PLANT.filter((p) => p.kind === 'breaker').length,
  disconnectors: PLANT.filter((p) => p.kind === 'disconnector').length,
  earths: PLANT.filter((p) => p.kind === 'earth').length,
  transformers: PLANT.filter((p) => p.kind === 'transformer').length,
  feeders: PLANT.filter((p) => p.kind === 'feeder').length,
};
---
<Base
  title="Your network model — Interlock"
  description="How Interlock builds a network model from what you already have, what it does when the model is wrong, and how a schedule stays tied to the version it was proved against."
  sheet="The network model"
  sheetNo="IL-300"
>
  <section class="open open--tight">
    <div class="sheet">
      <p class="label">Sheet IL-300</p>
      <h1 class="display display--sm">Your model is not good enough. That is the normal case.</h1>
      <p class="sub">
        Every network we have modelled had drawings that disagreed with each other, plant that had been
        replaced without the record changing, and at least one asset nobody could name. Interlock is
        built for that rather than around it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sheet">
      <div class="modelgrid">
        <div class="modelgrid__draw">
          <Diagram
            closed={normal.closed}
            energised={[...normal.energised]}
            earthed={[...normal.earthed]}
          />
          <p class="small modelgrid__cap">
            {SITE.designation} in its normal running arrangement: both transformers in service, each
            feeding its own 11kV section, bus section open. This is the drawing every step of every
            schedule on this site is rendered from.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="label">What a model is</p>
          <h2 class="head">Connectivity and state. Not geometry, not ratings, not protection.</h2>
          <div class="prose prose--tight">
            <p>
              A single-line diagram in Visio is a picture: it looks like the network and cannot be
              asked a question. What Interlock needs is smaller than a full asset register and larger
              than a drawing — every switching device, what it sits between, and whether it is normally
              open or closed.
            </p>
            <p>
              For Attercliffe that is {PLANT.length} items. It took two days to build from an existing
              drawing and a walk round the site with the district SAP, and one afternoon to correct
              afterwards.
            </p>
          </div>
          <dl class="counts">
            <div><dt>Circuit breakers</dt><dd class="mono">{counts.breakers}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Disconnectors</dt><dd class="mono">{counts.disconnectors}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Earth devices</dt><dd class="mono">{counts.earths}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Transformers</dt><dd class="mono">{counts.transformers}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Outgoing circuits</dt><dd class="mono">{counts.feeders}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Customers</dt><dd class="mono">{SITE.customersTotal.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sheet">
      <div class="split">
        <div>
          <p class="label">Being wrong</p>
          <h2 class="head">Where the model stops, Interlock stops.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The dangerous failure for a tool like this is not saying "I do not know". It is quietly
            assuming a point is open because nothing in the record says otherwise, and then proving a
            schedule against that assumption. An assumption and a proof are indistinguishable in the
            output right up until they are not.
          </p>
          <p>
            So unmodelled plant is recorded, and a sequence that reaches into it is refused rather than
            proved. The refusal names who holds the drawing that would close the gap, because that is
            the action it implies.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="unmodelled">
        <p class="unmodelled__k">Recorded as unmodelled · {SITE.modelVersion}</p>
        {SITE.unmodelled.map((entry) => (
          <div class="unmodelled__row">
            <p class="unmodelled__p">{entry.plant}</p>
            <p class="unmodelled__r">{entry.reason}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
        <p class="unmodelled__f">
          One item, on a network of {PLANT.length}. It is on the page rather than in a log because it is
          the sort of thing that decides whether a schedule can be proved next month.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="sheet">
      <div class="split">
        <div>
          <p class="label">Versions</p>
          <h2 class="head">A schedule is only meaningful against the model it was proved on.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The model changes: plant is replaced, a feeder is transferred, a normally-open point moves.
            Every schedule records the version it was proved against, and every version records what
            changed and who changed it.
          </p>
          <p>
            When a model changes, Interlock re-proves the schedules that touch the plant that moved and
            tells you which of them no longer hold. That is the check nobody currently does, because
            doing it by hand means re-reading every schedule in the drawer.
          </p>
          <p>
            A schedule proved eight months ago can be opened and shown in the network as it was
            understood that day — which is the form the question takes in an investigation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .open--tight { padding-block: clamp(var(--s7), 6vw, var(--s9)) clamp(var(--s7), 5vw, var(--s8)); }
  .display--sm { font-size: clamp(1.875rem, 1.1rem + 2.4vw, 3.25rem); max-width: 21ch; }
  .display + .sub { margin-top: var(--s5); }
  .split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.85fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--s8); align-items: start; }
  .label + .head { margin-top: var(--s3); }

  .modelgrid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--s8); align-items: start; }
  .modelgrid__draw { background: var(--sheet); border: var(--w-hair) solid var(--hair); padding: var(--s5); }
  .modelgrid__cap { margin-top: var(--s4); padding-top: var(--s4); border-top: var(--w-hair) solid var(--hair); }
  .prose--tight { margin-top: var(--s4); }

  .counts { margin: var(--s6) 0 0; border-top: var(--w-rule) solid var(--ink); }
  .counts > div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto; gap: var(--s4);
    padding-block: var(--s3); border-bottom: var(--w-hair) solid var(--hair); }
  .counts dt { font-size: var(--t-small); color: var(--ink-2); }
  .counts dd { margin: 0; font-size: var(--t-small); font-weight: 500; }

  .unmodelled { margin-top: var(--s7); border-left: 3px solid var(--risk); background: var(--risk-soft); padding: var(--s5); }
  .unmodelled__k { font-size: var(--t-micro); font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--risk); }
  .unmodelled__row { margin-top: var(--s4); padding-top: var(--s4);
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--risk) 25%, transparent); }
  .unmodelled__p { font-size: var(--t-body); font-weight: 500; }
  .unmodelled__r { margin-top: 4px; font-size: var(--t-small); color: var(--ink-2); }
  .unmodelled__f { margin-top: var(--s5); font-size: var(--t-small); color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 56ch; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .split, .modelgrid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--s5); }
    .modelgrid__draw { overflow-x: auto; }
    .modelgrid__draw :global(.sld) { min-width: 560px; }
    .modelgrid__cap { min-width: 0; }
  }
</style>
